 Critter: a roadside animal as a baked-polygon billboard.

 A POINT AND A VECTOR MAP DIFFERENTLY, and the chapter's own prose says that is
 the one thing here easy to get wrong. A gradient's linear endpoints and its
 radial CENTRE are points and translate to the feet anchor; its radial AXES are
 vectors and only scale and flip. Mapping an axis as a point anchors the ellipse's
 shape to the animal's feet, which looks like a shading bug and is a coordinate
 one. Both mappings are graded on the same inputs so the difference IS the test:
 the two answers differ by exactly the anchor.

 THE HEIGHT IS IN PIXELS, WHICH IS THE WHOLE TRICK. Project the feet, project a
 point `height` metres above them, and the gap is how tall the animal is on this
 frame -- so perspective is applied once, to a scalar, and every unit-frame
 coordinate then scales by that one number. A two-metre animal a thousand metres
 off through a focal of 500 is exactly one pixel tall, which is also the boundary
 of the sub-pixel bail.

 UNDER A PIXEL IT DRAWS NOTHING, and the test is a strict `< 1.0`, so an animal
 exactly one pixel tall is still drawn. Both sides are graded, because a `<=`
 there would silently drop the whole far rank of a herd.

 THE ART IS BAKED FACING LEFT, so an animal facing right mirrors in x -- and the
 mirror is a MINUS ONE rather than a flag threaded through every mapping. That is
 why `facing` answers a Real.

 We say:

Section: Facing

  facing-got : List Real
  facing-got = [facing True, facing False]

  facing-want : List Real
  facing-want = [-1.0, 1.0]

Section: Points And Vectors

 The anchor is the feet: 100 across, 200 down the screen. The animal is 50 pixels
 tall, so a unit-frame coordinate of a half is 25 pixels -- to the right in x and
 UP in y, which is why y subtracts.

  feet : ScreenPt
  feet = ScreenPt { x = 100.0, y = 200.0 }

  point-got : List Real
  point-got =
    let a = map-p feet 1.0 50.0 0.5 0.4
    in let b = map-p feet (0.0 - 1.0) 50.0 0.5 0.4
    in let c = map-p feet 1.0 50.0 0.0 0.0
    in [a.x, a.y, b.x, b.y, c.x, c.y]

  point-want : List Real
  point-want = [125.0, 180.0, 75.0, 180.0, 100.0, 200.0]

 The same numbers as a VECTOR: the scale and the mirror apply, the anchor does
 not. So a vector at the origin stays at the origin where a point lands on the
 feet.

  vector-got : List Real
  vector-got =
    let a = map-v 1.0 50.0 0.5 0.4
    in let b = map-v (0.0 - 1.0) 50.0 0.5 0.4
    in let c = map-v 1.0 50.0 0.0 0.0
    in [a.x, a.y, b.x, b.y, c.x, c.y]

  vector-want : List Real
  vector-want = [25.0, -20.0, -25.0, -20.0, 0.0, 0.0]

 The difference between the two IS the anchor, on every input.

  anchor-got : List Real
  anchor-got =
    let p = map-p feet 1.0 50.0 0.5 0.4
    in let v = map-v 1.0 50.0 0.5 0.4
    in [p.x - v.x, p.y - v.y]

  anchor-want : List Real
  anchor-want = [100.0, 200.0]

 A whole polygon maps point by point, in order.

  mapped-got : List Real
  mapped-got =
    let ps = mapped-pts feet 1.0 50.0 [StillPt { x = 0.0, y = 0.0 }, StillPt { x = 0.5, y = 0.4 }, StillPt { x = 1.0, y = 1.0 }] 0
    in [ real-from-int (list-length ps),
         (list-at ps 0).x, (list-at ps 0).y,
         (list-at ps 1).x, (list-at ps 1).y,
         (list-at ps 2).x, (list-at ps 2).y ]

  mapped-want : List Real
  mapped-want = [3.0, 100.0, 200.0, 125.0, 180.0, 150.0, 150.0]

Section: The Buffer Guard

 The zig's screen buffer holds 512 points and a longer polygon is SKIPPED rather
 than truncated, for the same reason `emit-ground` refuses its ninth point: a
 silently shortened polygon is a wrong shape drawn confidently.

  many : Integer -> List StillPt
  many (n) = if n <= 0 then [] else [StillPt { x = 0.0, y = 0.0 }] & many (n - 1)

  flat-poly : Integer -> StillPoly
  flat-poly (n) = StillPoly { color = 7, grad = [], pts = many n }

  guard-got : List Integer
  guard-got =
    [ max-critter-pts,
      list-length (critter-poly feet 1.0 50.0 (flat-poly 3)),
      list-length (critter-poly feet 1.0 50.0 (flat-poly 512)),
      list-length (critter-poly feet 1.0 50.0 (flat-poly 513)),
      list-length (critter-poly feet 1.0 50.0 (flat-poly 2)) ]

  guard-want : List Integer
  guard-want = [512, 1, 1, 0, 0]

 A polygon with no gradient is a flat fill, and it keeps its colour.

  solid-got : List Integer
  solid-got =
    let c = list-at (critter-poly feet 1.0 50.0 (flat-poly 3)) 0
    in [c.tag, c.color, list-length c.pts]

  solid-want : List Integer
  solid-want = [0, 7, 6]

Section: Drawing One

 The pixel height is the projected gap between the feet and the top, so a
 two-metre animal a thousand metres off through a focal of 500 is exactly one
 pixel -- the boundary, and it still draws. Twice as far and it is half a pixel
 and draws nothing at all.

  drawn : Real, Real -> Integer
  drawn (forward) (height) = list-length (critter-draw 0.0 forward height 128024 False 500.0 960.0)

  size-got : List Integer
  size-got =
    [ drawn 1000.0 2.0,
      drawn 2000.0 2.0,
      drawn 1000.0 1.9,
      drawn 100.0 2.0 ]

  size-want : List Integer
  size-want = [6, 0, 0, 6]

 An unknown codepoint has no art, so it emits nothing however big it is.

  unknown-got : List Integer
  unknown-got =
    [ list-length (emoji-polys-for 1),
      list-length (critter-draw 0.0 100.0 2.0 1 False 500.0 960.0),
      list-length (emoji-polys-for 128024) ]

  unknown-want : List Integer
  unknown-want = [0, 0, 6]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cr-facing" facing-got facing-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cr-point " point-got point-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cr-vector" vector-got vector-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cr-anchor" anchor-got anchor-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cr-mapped" mapped-got mapped-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cr-guard " guard-got guard-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cr-solid " solid-got solid-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cr-size  " size-got size-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cr-unknwn" unknown-got unknown-want)
  end
